# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLIENT DATA. This is the important half of this file.
#
# The tools write a report next to themselves for every machine they run
# on, and those reports contain the machine name, make, model, SERIAL
# NUMBER, installed software and event log entries for somebody else's
# computer. One of the reports on the stick this came from named a real
# person's laptop and its serial.
#
# Nothing here that matches a report may ever be committed, and that
# matters more than usual because a checkout of this repo IS a working
# toolkit: you run it, it writes reports, and the next `git add -A`
# would sweep them in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
report-*.txt
report-*.md
repairlog-*.txt
*-report-*.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIRD-PARTY BINARIES. Fetched by Get-Tools.ps1, never committed.
#
# Not a size decision, a licensing one. These are other people's
# programs and most are not ours to redistribute. Hiren's BootCD PE
# contains Windows PE, which is Microsoft licensed. Each tool is
# downloaded from its own official source instead, so every publisher
# keeps control of their own distribution.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tools/* not Tools/, and the negation below is why. Git cannot re-include
# a file if a PARENT DIRECTORY of it is excluded, so a bare `Tools/` would
# make `!Tools/HealthReport/` do nothing at all and silently untrack the
# main tool the moment it moved in here.
# Tools/ holds BOTH our own scripts and the third-party utilities that
# are never committed. So the folder CONTENTS are excluded and our own
# files re-included by extension. A bare `Tools/` would make these
# negations do nothing: git cannot re-include a file if a PARENT
# DIRECTORY of it is excluded.
Tools/*
!Tools/*.ps1
!Tools/*.bat
ISO/
Setup/
*.iso
*.exe
*.msi
*.zip
*.7z
*.wim

# BIOS and firmware images. Machine specific, large, and dangerous if
# flashed to the wrong board.
*.bin
*.rom
*.cap
BIOS-*/

# EXTRACTED ICONS, a cache and not a source.
#
# Each tool's icon is now read out of the tool's own binary by
# Tools\Get-Icons.ps1, named by slug, and written here. It is rebuilt
# from whatever is actually plugged in, so committing it would ship 37
# pictures of programs that are not in this repo, and adding a tool
# would once again mean drawing it an icon by hand.
Icons/*.png
# Except the three that are OURS. Health Report, Restore Point and
# Activity Log are our own scripts: they have no binary for the
# extractor to read, so their artwork is a source file like any other.
!Icons/healthreportandrepair.png
!Icons/restorepoint.png
!Icons/activitylog.png

# WINDOWS SHORTCUTS. A .lnk is a binary file with an ABSOLUTE PATH
# baked into it. The one on the stick points at "D:\Lazarus.hta" with
# a working directory of "D:\", so it is correct on exactly one machine
# with exactly one drive letter and silently wrong everywhere else.
#
# It is excluded by name rather than left to chance, because the
# checkpoint hook stages with `git add -A` and would otherwise sweep it
# in the first time somebody plugged the stick in at a different letter.
*.lnk

# PER-STICK STATE, not project state.
#
# The ADMIN slider records its OFF state as a file. Committing that
# would ship a clone with elevation silently switched off, on the very
# machines that need it, and the slider would then be telling the truth
# about a setting nobody here chose. It is a property of one stick.
Docs/admin-off.flag

# Windows and editor noise
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
*~
.vscode/
.idea/

# Anything that looks like a credential, as a backstop. Nothing here
# should ever produce one, which is exactly when it slips through.
*.env
.env
*.pfx
*.key
*_rsa
id_rsa*
secrets.*

# Real client names for Tests\check-privacy.ps1. Never committed: a
# checker that hardcodes the names it protects has published them.
Tests/private-names.txt
